From: Mehmet Salih <salihmehmet497@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] mt76: mt7915: fix NULL list_head crash when reset_work fires before probe completes

On real hardware where mt7915_mcu_init() (the firmware/MCU load
handshake) times out ("Failed to get patch semaphore"), probe fails
and returns before mt76_register_device() is ever reached -- so
mt76_phy_init() never runs and phy->tx_list is left as raw zeroed
memory (next/prev == NULL). reset_work, however, is INIT_WORK'd
earlier in mt7915_register_device(), before the firmware init call,
and a hardware watchdog interrupt reacting to firmware never coming
up can schedule it anyway.

list_empty()'s "next == self" invariant does not hold for
zero-initialized memory, so mt76_txq_schedule_pending()'s early-exit
guard is silently bypassed, and list_splice_init() dereferences the
NULL list_head. Confirmed on real ARM32 hardware: NULL pointer write
at fault address 0x4 (offset of struct list_head's "prev" on this
32-bit target), call trace mt7915_mac_reset_work ->
mt7915_mac_full_reset -> mt7915_mac_restart ->
mt76_txq_schedule_all(&dev->mphy) -> mt76_txq_schedule_pending,
PC at mt76_txq_schedule_pending+0x5c.

Two complementary fixes:

 1. Initialize phy->tx_list/tx_lock in mt76_alloc_device(), at the
    very start of the device's lifetime, instead of only in
    mt76_phy_init() (reached much later, after firmware init has
    already succeeded). This closes the invariant-violation window
    regardless of which early-triggered path touches phy->tx_list
    first. mt76_phy_init()'s own (now redundant) init is left in
    place -- re-initializing an empty, not-yet-used list is
    harmless.

 2. Bail out of mt7915_mac_reset_work() immediately if the device
    was never fully registered (MT76_STATE_REGISTERED unset) --
    every code path in that function assumes a fully-up device
    (mac80211 hw, tx_worker, napi, wcid/tx_list state, ...), and a
    reset cannot usefully do anything for a device that never
    finished probing in the first place.

Does not fix the underlying firmware semaphore timeout itself (WiFi
still won't come up) -- this only prevents the resulting watchdog
recovery attempt from crashing the kernel and causing a reboot loop.

--- a/mac80211.c
+++ b/mac80211.c
@@ -690,6 +690,34 @@ mt76_alloc_device(struct device *pdev,
 	phy->band_idx = MT_BAND0;
 	dev->phys[phy->band_idx] = phy;

+	/*
+	 * phy->tx_list/tx_lock are otherwise first initialized in
+	 * mt76_phy_init(), which only runs once mt76_register_device()
+	 * is reached -- i.e. after the driver's own firmware/MCU init
+	 * (e.g. mt7915_mcu_init()) has already succeeded. If that
+	 * firmware handshake times out, probe fails and returns before
+	 * mt76_register_device() ever runs, leaving phy->tx_list as raw
+	 * zeroed memory (next/prev == NULL). That doesn't satisfy
+	 * list_empty()'s "next == self" invariant, so a still-armed
+	 * hardware-watchdog-triggered reset_work (INIT_WORK'd earlier in
+	 * probe, before the same firmware init call) can walk straight
+	 * past mt76_txq_schedule_pending()'s list_empty() guard into
+	 * list_splice_init(), dereferencing a NULL list_head -- observed
+	 * on real hardware as a NULL pointer write in
+	 * mt76_txq_schedule_pending, reached via mt7915_mac_reset_work ->
+	 * mt7915_mac_full_reset -> mt7915_mac_restart ->
+	 * mt76_txq_schedule_all(&dev->mphy), fault address 0x4 (offset
+	 * of struct list_head's "prev" on this 32-bit ARM target).
+	 * Initializing here, at the very start of the device's
+	 * lifetime -- before any firmware/probe work that could fail --
+	 * closes that window regardless of which early-triggered path
+	 * ends up touching phy->tx_list first. mt76_phy_init()'s own
+	 * (now redundant) init is left in place; re-initializing an
+	 * empty, not-yet-used list is harmless.
+	 */
+	INIT_LIST_HEAD(&phy->tx_list);
+	spin_lock_init(&phy->tx_lock);
+
 	spin_lock_init(&dev->rx_lock);
 	spin_lock_init(&dev->lock);
 	spin_lock_init(&dev->cc_lock);

--- a/mt7915/mac.c
+++ b/mt7915/mac.c
@@ -1478,6 +1478,25 @@ void mt7915_mac_reset_work(struct work_
 	int i;

 	dev = container_of(work, struct mt7915_dev, reset_work);
+
+	/*
+	 * reset_work is INIT_WORK'd (mt7915_register_device()) before the
+	 * driver's firmware/MCU init (mt7915_init_hardware() ->
+	 * mt7915_mcu_init()) runs, and a hardware watchdog interrupt can
+	 * schedule it in response to firmware never coming up -- i.e.
+	 * this can fire before mt76_register_device() (and therefore
+	 * MT76_STATE_REGISTERED) is ever reached, on a device that never
+	 * finished probing. Every path below assumes a fully registered
+	 * device (mac80211 hw, tx_worker, napi, wcid/tx_list state, ...);
+	 * running any of it against that half-initialized state is what
+	 * produced a NULL list_head dereference in
+	 * mt76_txq_schedule_pending() on real hardware. Bail out here
+	 * instead -- there is nothing a *reset* can usefully do for a
+	 * device that never successfully started in the first place.
+	 */
+	if (!test_bit(MT76_STATE_REGISTERED, &dev->mphy.state))
+		return;
+
 	ext_phy = dev->mt76.phys[MT_BAND1];
 	phy2 = ext_phy ? ext_phy->priv : NULL;
